WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.070
We have been covering some outstanding soldiers
here at Biographics, lately. Today’s protagonist

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:08.530
is no exception and would probably rank among
the very top because of his determination,

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.570
courage, tactical competence and resilience.
This is the extraordinary story of Master

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:20.470
Sergeant Raul ‘Roy’ Benavidez, the Lazarus
soldier, and of his three lives. This outstanding

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.900
individual never surrendered in the face of
adversity, from childhood to military life.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.080
Those who know his story mostly remember him
for a six hour Calvary, during which, to great

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:34.280
personal sacrifice he held at bay an entire
battalion of North Vietnamese infantry to

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.440
save eight wounded comrades. But how did he
get there? And what happened afterwards?

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.920
Let’s begin with a difficult childhood in
Texas …

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:57.910
Young Roy
Raul Perez Benavidez, also known as ‘Roy’

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:03.200
was born in Cuero, Texas, on the 5th of August
1935. Roy was born in a family of Mexican

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.490
Americans established in DeWitt county, Texas.
Roy’s father, Salvador, worked as a sharecropper.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.430
He was married to Teresa Perez, an American
Yaqui Indian. The childhood of Roy was not

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:19.390
a happy one. On the 7th of November 1938,
Salvador died of tuberculosis, aged only 27.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.189
Roy was only three.
Four years later, Teresa also died, again

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:27.820
from tuberculosis. Roy and his younger brother
Roger were sent to live with their Uncle Nicholas,

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.000
Aunt Alejandra and eight cousins at El Campo,
Texas.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.110
Roy dropped out of school during the seventh
grade: his help was needed to support the

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.610
family. So, he had to forego an education
to work in the fields, picking sugar beets

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.369
and cotton.
Texas in the early ‘50s did not offer many

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:48.200
prospects to a farm labourer who was also
a school dropout. Roy found a chance for a

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.950
career, and a better future, in the military.
In 1952, aged 17, Roy enlisted in the Texas

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:58.520
National Guard. Two years later Roy joined
the US Army as Private Benavidez, receiving

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.400
his infantry training at Fort Ord, California.
Benavidez service duties soon took him out

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.710
of the country, first to South Korea and then
Germany. Upon his return to the States in

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.890
1959 he reunited with Hilaria Coy, a childhood
sweetheart, and the two married on the 7th

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.790
of June. They went on to have three children:
Noel, Yvette and Denise.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.630
By now Army life for Benavidez was not only
a means to provide for his family, it was

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.100
‘life’, pure and simple, a life that he
enjoyed and was very good at. He expanded

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.010
his training and in 1959 he attended the military
police school in Fort Gordon, Georgia.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:35.240
Benavidez racked up qualifications, becoming
also a paratrooper and was assigned to the

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.940
82nd Airborne Division.
By 1965 Benavidez was on a tour of duty in

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.910
Vietnam, acting as a military adviser to the
South Vietnamese Army.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:47.150
One day he was on a solo patrol mission, which
according to some accounts was part of a covert

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.720
operation: its aim was to gather intelligence
of possible Vietcong infiltration within the

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.420
South Vietnamese Army.
That was a fateful day. Benavidez stepped

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.880
on a land mine and the deflagration left him
badly wounded, with serious bone and cartilage

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.980
damage to his spine. He was immediately evacuated
to Brooke Army Hospital in San Antonio, then

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.720
to Fort Sam, Houston.
The Army doctors gave him a devastating diagnosis:

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.590
he would never walk again, meaning they had
to discharge him from the military.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.420
They might as well thrown him on top of another
mine. To him and his family, active service

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:25.120
meant making a living, it meant ‘life’.
A discharge was equivalent to death.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.060
Against the wall
What Roy Benavidez did in the following weeks

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.650
would have been described as a miracle, in
another place and another time. Ignoring medical

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:39.570
advice, Benavidez went on a recovery programme
on his own, in secret. Every night, he slipped

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.570
from his bed and on to the floor. Using his
elbows and chin, he dragged his body to the

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:48.560
nearest wall. Then, in a pain so excruciating
that left him in tears, Roy would set himself

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.110
up against the wall and propped himself to
a standing position.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.780
He then spent the night trying to just wiggle
his toes, right foot, left foot, and then

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.380
again. Every single night. He was determined
to walk again and to return to Vietnam. He

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:05.380
felt a sense of duty towards his fellow comrades,
many of whom came back from their tours of

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.030
duty with limbs missing, truly unable to walk
again.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.350
Night after night, Benavidez continued with
his ritual. Sometimes he was caught by the

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:16.450
nurses, given some sleeping pills, and put
back to bed. But most nights he managed to

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.150
keep the appointment with the wall, and he
recovered most of his mobility.

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:23.810
After nine months, a doctor showed up with
his discharge papers. Benavidez told him to

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.720
hold on and to watch what he was able to do:
Roy jumped out of bed and stood in front of

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.970
the doctor, almost fainting because of his
back pain.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.660
The doctor was impressed and told him
“Benavidez, if you walk out of this room,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.960
I will tear the papers!”
And Benavidez walked out of the room, out

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.090
of the hospital. Like St Lazarus, he had walked
out of his grave and into the start of his

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:51.130
second life.
Tango Mike Mike: Six hours in hell

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.030
After leaving the hospital Roy Benavidez spent
months of gruelling rehabilitation and training

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:59.020
at Fort Bragg, North Carolina. This is the
main training centre for the US Army Special

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.230
Forces, also known as Green Berets.
He entered Fort Bragg with a limp. Soon, he

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:08.340
was running five to ten miles and dropping
1500 push ups every single day, while perfecting

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.310
his parachuting skills.
In January of 1968 Staff Sergeant Roy Benavidez

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:17.530
was back in Vietnam. He had been assigned
to Detachment B56, 5th Special Forces Airborne

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:22.370
Group, with code name ‘Tango Mike Mike’.
In NATO radio code these words simply indicate

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:27.620
the letters T – M – M. Apparently Benavidez
himself had selected these letters, an acronym

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.400
which stood for
‘That Mean Mexican’

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.730
It would take only a couple of months before
the Mean Mexican launched into the military

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:37.850
action that would eventually earn him a Medal
of Honour. He would later describe what happened

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.120
as
‘Six hours in hell’.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:45.330
On the 2nd of May 1968, Benavidez was at the
Loc Ninh Green Beret outpost, near the Cambodian

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:51.490
border, attending Mass. At 1:30 PM, the radio
came alive with a panicked request for help.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:56.290
It came from a squad of 12 men, a Reconnaissance
Team that had been dropped by helicopter west

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:01.200
of Loc Ninh, tasked with collecting intelligence
on enemy activity. The team included nine

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.210
Vietnamese Montagnards, or ‘Mountain Men’.
These are an ethnic group which had sided

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:09.800
with US and South Vietnam against the Communist
North. The team also included 3 US service

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:14.430
men: Sergeant First Class Leroy Wright, Staff
Sergeant Lloyd “Frenchie” Mousseau and

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.120
Specialist Four Brian O’Connor.
[Subtitle: A ‘Specialist’ in the US Army

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:17.120
ranks above a Private First Class and below
a Corporal]

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.020
The patrol had stumbled upon an entire infantry
battalion of the North Vietnam People’s

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.639
Army, the NVA, numbering up to 1,000 men.
They had been quickly surrounded. Three helicopters

00:06:25.639 --> 00:06:29.210
had already attempted to extract them, but
intense enemy fire had prevented them from

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.610
landing.
Benavidez was listening with increased concern

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:35.690
to the radio. He knew the men involved, and
especially Sgt Leroy Wright was a good friend.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:40.210
Back in April, Benavidez and another comrade
were on an intelligence mission deep beyond

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:44.150
enemy lines, when the other soldier had been
wounded. A helicopter had come to extract

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:48.021
them. Unable to land, the chopper had lowered
two ropes for the men to hook onto. During

00:06:48.021 --> 00:06:52.860
the flight, the two ropes were badly tangled
and were about to break. Sgt Wright, who was

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.980
on the helicopter had lowered himself with
another rope, disentangled the knot, and had

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:00.470
saved Benavidez’ life.
Tango Mike Mike took a snap decision: he would

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.030
help Leroy and the others. They would make
it back alive.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.090
As a fourth rescue helicopter was being readied
for another attempt, Benavidez jumped on board,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:12.930
armed only with a knife and his medical kit.
The chopper reached the site of the engagement:

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.080
the reconnaissance team was surrounded by
the NVA soldiers. Enemy fire prevented an

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:21.290
immediate landing, so the aircraft had to
move 75 yards away from Leroy Wright and his

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:22.290
men.
[Subtitle: 75 yards = 68.65 metres]

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:26.240
Benavidez jumped to the ground and run towards
the men, when a Vietnamese bullet hit him

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.110
in his right leg. He fell but was immediately
up and running.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:34.710
Next, it was the full blast of a grenade to
slam him to the ground, his face covered in

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:39.470
shrapnel. That, too, was not enough to stop
him: he got up again and reached the patrol,

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:43.150
that had split into two groups.
Four of the Montagnards lay dead. He took

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:47.560
one of their AK assault rifles and then focused
on treating the wounded. Luckily, he had brought

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.360
along plenty of morphine for those who were
screaming in pain.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.720
The only way to stop the overwhelming NVA
battalion was to call in an air strike. Benavidez

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.850
did so via radio, also requesting another
helicopter. Another bullet pierced his right

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.330
thigh.
Thankfully, the oncoming chopper succeeded

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:07.330
to land and Benavidez dragged the dead and
wounded on board. The NVA soldiers intensified

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:12.419
their fire against the aircraft, which prompted
another act of sacrifice from Benavidez:

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:16.590
Rather than jumping aboard, he ordered the
pilot to fly to the second group, while he

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:20.889
provided suppressive fire from the ground.
Benavidez spotted Leroy Wright. He was on

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:24.250
the ground, not far from the second group.
Dead.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.960
Instinctively, Benavidez removed from Wright’s
body all documents containing their unit’s

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.650
radio codes and call signs. These codes would
have made for useful intelligence in the hands

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:37.880
of the NVA.
Then everything went wrong, way more wrong.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.560
First, a bullet hit him in the stomach, the
third shot he had to endure so far. Then,

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.050
a grenade exploded behind his back, with more
shrapnel piercing his flesh. Worse still,

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.150
the pilot was shot dead, and the helicopter
crashed.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.770
Benavidez limped back to the wreckage and
was able to extract his wounded comrades.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.700
Their only way to safety was to call in a
second air strike and a further chopper. But

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.310
first, he quickly set up a defence perimeter,
all the while encouraging, directing and caring

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:04.980
for the wounded men. Throughout the fighting,
Benavidez, a devout Catholic, made the sign

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.300
of the cross so many times that his arms
"were going like an airplane propeller”

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:13.490
Jets and helicopter gunships finally returned
to harass the enemy. By this time, Benavidez

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.740
was almost rendered blind from all the blood
in his eyes, so he had to rely on his sense

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:22.810
of hearing to direct the airstrikes.
The new rescue chopper finally landed. Benavidez

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:26.820
carried his comrades to the safety of the
helicopter, one at the time. During the second

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.800
trip, while he was shoving ‘Frenchie’
Mousseau aboard, an NVA soldier closed up

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.900
to him and clubbed him on the jaw with the
butt of his rifle.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:38.320
Benavidez fell, then got up, just in time
to stop the soldier’s bayonet … with his

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.890
bare right hand. The Vietnamese managed to
slash also his left forearm, before Benavidez

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:46.380
fought back with his Bowie knife, stabbing
him multiple times.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.400
After having secured ‘Frenchie’ on board,
Benavidez spotted two more NVA troops advancing,

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:55.220
shooting towards the chopper. He grabbed an
AK-47 and fired at them, silencing them for

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.300
good.
His mission was not finished. With little

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.200
strength remaining in his body Benavidez rushed
back to the defence perimeter to rescue the

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.200
last Montagnard and collect all classified
documents.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:08.810
Benavidez was in critical condition.
He was bleeding from numerous wounds.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:13.779
He was blinded by haemorrhage in his eyes.
He had a dislocated jaw.

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:18.330
His intestines were exposed because of a gun
shot in the abdomen. He had to hold them in

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:22.060
with his hand.
But everybody else was safe, so he allowed

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:27.200
himself to be pulled into the chopper. On
route to Loc Ninh, Benavidez closed his eyes.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:31.149
When the chopper landed at the field hospital,
the medics tended immediately to the wounded

00:10:31.149 --> 00:10:34.560
and collected the bodies of the dead soldiers.
One at the time, they were placed in the ordnance

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.060
black plastic body bags.
Amongst them, there was Staff Sergeant Raul

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:40.450
‘Roy’ Benavidez, code name ‘Tango Mike
Mike’.

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:45.180
[Suggested edit: gradual fade to black, then
transition to next title card:]

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:49.820
The Third Life of Sgt Benavidez
Jerry Cottingham, a friend, recognized Roy’s

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:52.990
body and screamed: "That's Benavidez! Get
a doc!"

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:56.580
When the doctor arrived, he placed his hand
on Roy's chest to check for a heartbeat. He

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:00.020
shook his head, saying
"There's nothing I can do for him."

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:04.030
The doctor proceeded to zip up the body bag.
Half of Sgt Benavidez’ body had already

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:08.240
been swallowed by the sinister black bag.
Then, the doctor closing the zipper had the

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:12.070
surprise of his life. The body of Benavidez
… spat at him!

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:15.360
The surprised doctor reversed Roy's condition
from ‘dead’ to:

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.779
"He won't make it, but we'll try".
Roy had not surrendered. He was in critical

00:11:19.779 --> 00:11:24.370
condition having suffered more than 30 wounds.
He could not talk because of the broken jaw

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:27.279
and his eyes had been sealed shut by congealed
blood.

00:11:27.279 --> 00:11:32.779
But Benavidez was still alive! Unable to communicate
otherwise, he had spat to attract the doctor’s

00:11:32.779 --> 00:11:36.400
attention, and it had worked!
Staff Sgt Benavidez had saved the lives of

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:41.660
at least eight men to great personal sacrifice.
He spent almost a year in hospitals to recover

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:44.570
from his injuries.
He had seven major gunshot wounds, one of

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:48.930
them having barely missed the heart.
Both arms had been slashed by a bayonet.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:53.589
Benavidez had shrapnel in his head, scalp,
shoulder, buttocks, feet, and legs.

00:11:53.589 --> 00:11:57.779
His right lung was destroyed. He had injuries
to his mouth and back of his head from being

00:11:57.779 --> 00:12:01.279
clubbed with a rifle butt.
The Medal of Honour citation would later acknowledge

00:12:01.279 --> 00:12:03.490
that:
“His fearless personal leadership, tenacious

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:08.839
devotion to duty, and extremely valorous actions
in the face of overwhelming odds were in keeping

00:12:08.839 --> 00:12:14.130
with the highest traditions of the military
service and reflect the utmost credit on him

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:22.520
and the United States Army.”

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.740
How did he do it?
How did Benavidez manage to accomplish these

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.770
actions, which are short of miraculous? How
do soldiers, and other individuals in high

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:34.770
risk situations, accomplish feats that appear
super-human?

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:38.770
Benavidez later claimed that he was simply
doing his duty. And to survive six hours in

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:43.760
hell, he clearly had a strong sense of duty:
he was motivated to protect his friends and

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:48.650
to save the classified documents. Losing the
intelligence may have endangered further lives.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:53.300
In addition to a strong sense of purpose,
Roy Benavidez was no fresh recruit. He was

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:58.660
an experienced sergeant of the Green Berets,
the Special Forces of the US Army. In the

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:03.350
1960s their typical training included parachuting,
marksmanship, close quarter combat, and extensive

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:07.350
medical first-aid drills – which made of
virtually every Green Beret a trained paramedic.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:11.620
A skill which Benavidez put to good use.
And yet, what is really astounding is how

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:17.080
he managed to simply stay standing up after
six hours of exhausting, non-stop action and

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:22.270
a total of 36 wounds which must have caused
excruciating pain.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:25.850
He did administer morphine to his comrades,
but not to himself, otherwise he would not

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:30.240
have had the lucidity necessary to plan on
the spot the defensive perimeter, to retrieve

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:36.670
the intel, to fight back against the NVA soldiers.
The answer may lie in another drug, one created

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:41.290
by the human body: adrenaline.
The word ‘adrenaline’ today is used almost

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:47.350
as an abstract term to indicate an extremely
excitable and alert state of being. In practical

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:51.709
terms, adrenaline is a hormone and neurotransmitter
produced in the centre of the adrenal glands.

00:13:51.709 --> 00:13:56.790
Adrenaline is released into the bloodstream
and conveys nerve impulses to various organs.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:01.540
Its effects are to prepare the body for the
‘fight or flight’ response in times of

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:06.410
extreme stress. In other words, it sets the
body up for vigorous, sudden action.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:10.709
The key effects of the onset of adrenaline
include increased heart rate and blood pressure,

00:14:10.709 --> 00:14:15.589
expansion of the air passages, and redistribution
of blood to the muscles. This explains the

00:14:15.589 --> 00:14:20.620
increased strength, speed, stamina and pain
threshold experienced by individuals who like

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:25.480
Benavidez find themselves in a life and death
scenario. The effects are not limited to the

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:30.580
body, though: this hormone also maximises
blood glucose levels, increasing its concentrations

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:33.760
primarily in the brain.
This is like providing a sudden boost of fuel

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.530
to one’s cognitive function, which enabled
Benavidez to sustain six hours of intense

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:44.279
fighting, but most of all six hours of brilliant
tactical leadership and medical and evacuation

00:14:44.279 --> 00:14:47.870
work.
What was the reward for his actions?

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.800
Medal of Honour [TA1]
Staff Sergeant Benavidez was absolutely worthy

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:55.340
of receiving the highest US Army accolade,
the Congressional Medal of Honour. But this

00:14:55.340 --> 00:15:00.230
did not happen immediately. As he was recovering
from his wounds, in 1968, he was awarded the

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:05.680
Distinguished Service Cross, also prestigious,
but not as much as ‘The’ Medal. But the

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:10.520
process to obtain the Cross was shorter, and
his commanding officer was not sure if Benavidez

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:14.089
would survive.
When Roy Benavidez, after one year in hospital,

00:15:14.089 --> 00:15:18.269
returned to active service, his new commander
Colonel Ralph Drake undertook a campaign to

00:15:18.269 --> 00:15:22.570
ensure that he received the Medal of Honour.
But there were two problems at this stage.

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:26.760
First: there wasn’t any witness from the
US military that could confirm the full extent

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:29.510
of his actions.
Second: according to Benavidez’ children,

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:34.360
the process took so long because the action
had taken place in Cambodian territory. The

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:38.570
Government could not admit to taking part
in such engagements in a Country not involved

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:43.610
in the Vietnam war, at least nominally.
Finally, in 1980 a witness emerged, it was

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:47.149
Brian O’Connor, the Specialist, who had
survived the battle. He had been living in

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:50.490
the Fiji, but during a trip to Australia he
heard about the Benavidez case.

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:54.280
O’Connor gladly volunteered to write a full
report on Roy’s actions and then flew to

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:56.180
Washington to present it in person to the
Government.

00:15:56.180 --> 00:16:01.310
At last, on the 24th of February 1981, President
Reagan presented the Medal of Honour to Roy

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:05.810
Benavidez. He was the last of the Vietnam-era
military to be presented with a medal from

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:13.790
that war.
One last fight[TA2]

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:17.690
During the past decade Benavidez had rose
to the rank of Master Sergeant and had sought

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:21.790
to advance his education, attaining his GED
and then a college degree. During all this

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:24.670
time he suffered from chronic pain from his
injuries.

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:30.010
In 1976, Roy Benavidez decided to retire from
the Army, and was granted some well-deserved

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:34.880
disability payments on top of his pension.
When President Reagan had presented Roy Benavidez

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.269
with the Medal of Honour, he had asked the
former sergeant to speak to young people about

00:16:38.269 --> 00:16:41.470
the importance of pursuing education.
Roy did just that: the best part of his time

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:45.900
as a retired Sergeant, he spent it visiting
schools to stress the need for the education

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:50.130
he never had as a child. He also became a
prolific motivational speaker, delivering

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:55.510
talks about his extraordinary life experiences
to audiences of students, military personnel

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.900
and businesses.
But his days of fighting were not done.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:03.970
Shortly before Memorial Day 1983, Roy Benavidez
released statements to warn that the Social

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:08.789
Security Administration planned to cut off
disability payments to him and other Vietnam

00:17:08.789 --> 00:17:11.559
War veterans.
A Federal cost-cutting plan had terminated

00:17:11.559 --> 00:17:16.889
the disability assistance for 350,000 people
over the preceding two years. It was now impacting

00:17:16.889 --> 00:17:20.600
also Benavidez.
The retired Sergeant had developed a friendship

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:24.970
with Ronald Reagan after being decorated.
He would not shy away from leveraging it.

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:27.139
He said
“I don't like to use my Medal of Honour

00:17:27.139 --> 00:17:31.509
for political purposes or personal gain, but
if they can do this to me, what will they

00:17:31.509 --> 00:17:34.960
do to all the others?''
10 days after Roy’s statements to the press,

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:39.679
the Secretary of Health and Human Services,
Margaret M. Heckler, said the disability reviews

00:17:39.679 --> 00:17:42.710
would become more
''humane and compassionate.''

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:46.870
Benavidez continued his campaign: he testified
to the House Select Committee on Aging that

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:51.489
the same administration that had given him
a medal, was now withdrawing his benefits,

00:17:51.489 --> 00:17:55.510
which were part of his rights as a wounded
veteran. He then proceeded to file an appeal

00:17:55.510 --> 00:17:59.529
to an Administrative Law Court.
In July 1983, the Court overturned the initial

00:17:59.529 --> 00:18:04.850
decision and sanctioned that Benavidez continue
receiving payments. It was an important precedent

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:08.519
that would help many more veterans.
From then on, Benavidez continued his engagements

00:18:08.519 --> 00:18:12.909
as a speaker in support of education and on
overcoming impossible odds.

00:18:12.909 --> 00:18:16.929
By all accounts he was a loving father and
grandfather. He did enforce quite a rigorous

00:18:16.929 --> 00:18:21.519
discipline at home, based on his Catholic
faith, and he must have appeared intimidating

00:18:21.519 --> 00:18:24.529
to his daughters’ boyfriends. But as daughter
Yvonne described him

00:18:24.529 --> 00:18:30.129
‘He was the biggest teddy bear in life’
In October of 1998, after many years of struggle

00:18:30.129 --> 00:18:34.559
against diabetes, Benavidez had his right
leg amputated, due to complications of the

00:18:34.559 --> 00:18:40.139
disease. He never recovered fully, and on
the 29th of November, aged 63, Roy Benavidez

00:18:40.139 --> 00:18:44.620
succumbed to respiratory failure at an Army
Hospital in San Antonio, Texas.

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:49.009
Over the years, fellow Texans paid tribute
to Benavidez. Several schools, a National

00:18:49.009 --> 00:18:53.299
Guard armoury, Army Reserve centre and even
a cargo of the Navy have been named after

00:18:53.299 --> 00:18:56.580
him.
Roy Benavidez life was one of struggle, sacrifice

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:02.040
and rebirth – not once, but twice. And yet,
he did not regard himself as someone special.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:06.419
''The real heroes are the ones who gave their
lives for their country. I don't like to be

00:19:06.419 --> 00:19:23.899
called a hero. I just did what I was trained

00:19:23.899 --> 00:19:26.889
to do.''

